A||||4||SLYGUY||Chargins system problems!!||||8||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||09-09-2001||01:18 AM||||This ****  blows!!<P>I was driving home from an auto parts store with some replacement bulbs for the instrument panel and the car started running a little weird. It wanted to cough, had very little power and I had to nurse it home. <BR>Shut the car off and it would not restart. You know the symptoms...<BR>lights dim, starter clicks, etc. etc. <P>Well, at first I thought that I had a bum battery, but after I jump started it I took a look at the Alt guage and it was reading around 11-12 volts!!<BR>Alternator is sick. Gonna replace it tomorrow. <BR>Anyone ever done that before?<BR>Any tips?<P>When I was changing oil and filters I saw the alternator. From the BOTTOM of the car. This looks like it's gonna be a ***** .<BR>I'm gonna jack the car up with a combination of a jack and jackstands and remove the right rear wheel. Is it a good idea to pull out the wheel well for added visibility/working room?<BR>Any special tools/procedures not covered in my ol' Chilton's manual?<P>Thanks again,<BR>Sly<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.printroom.com/_vti_bin/ViewImage.dll?userid=SLYGUY&album_id=35876&image_id=0&courtesy=1">  <P>88GT <BR>all stock...||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||09-09-2001||02:42 AM||||I haven't had to replace my alternator, but I hear that the automatics are more difficult than the 5 speed. Of all the years, 88s are easier because they don't have the cooling tubes for the air blower, that the older ones had. Probably will be easier with the wheel and splash shield (NOT the wheel well liner) removed. Some folks even suggest disconnecting one end of the dog bone, removing the two rear cradle bolts, and letting the rear (closest to the bumper) of the cradle down with a floor jack.<BR>If you decide to lower the cradle, be sure it is supported before you remove any bolts.<BR>(Did I even need to say that?)<P>One thing I noticed on my first Formula, when I was adjusting the alternator to tighten the belt... The top bolt will probably be <I>really</I> hard to turn. I've heard that this is not too uncommon. I had to use an impact wrench   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> to loosen it.<P>Sorry for your misfortune. Welcome to the wonderful world of Fiero ownership.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>It's worth it.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||varnster||09-09-2001||06:01 PM||||It is a bear cat on 2.8 auto trany, so before<BR>you decide to pull that thing make sure  ALL conections to the alt are in good repair <BR>no dirt or oil on the plug in portion and the<BR>nut is secure on the other wire, allso when you pull it {if needed} go to a rebuild shop<BR>and get a few more amps, it cost a touch more,$15 bucks or so, but you will have the extra reserve, for other goodies you might install over the years.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||09-09-2001||07:59 PM||||loosen the alternator then take the fan out of the alt,since you don't have the cooling tubes take it out from the top,if that don't work, jack the car up take the wheel off and run it thru the bottom,it's not that hard just take your time.AL||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||09-10-2001||12:09 AM||||I changed it today. I thought that it was gonna be a torture test, but it turned out to be real easy. I jacked it up from the rear, put a jack stand under a frame member just forward of the right rear wheel. <P>Then I moved the jack to a better spot and removed the wheel. This made it much easier because you could see practically every thing belt driven. I removed the belt tension bolt, removed the upper mount bolt, unplugged it and dropped it out the bottom. <P>Local parts house had it in stock. $109 after tax and took it home. Whole job took about 2-3 hours. Easier then I thought. <BR>As easy as it was, I'm gonna have my local Pontiac dealer change the belts along with diagnosing/repairing a check engine light problem. Possibly a sensor or a vacuum leak. Should come back in great shape. <BR>I'm still waiting for the headlight motor rebuild kit that I ordered and there won't be much left. Knock on wood!<P>Sly<BR>||||reg||14
